<article class="env-lemma" id="0DUY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DUY">Lemma <span data-tag="0DUY">106.14.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{X}$ be an algebraic stack. Assume $\mathcal{I}_\mathcal {X} \to \mathcal{X}$ is finite. Let $f : \mathcal{X} \to M$ be the moduli space constructed in Theorem <a href="/tag/0DUT" data-tag="0DUT">106.13.9</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $\mathcal{X}$ is quasi-separated, then $M$ is quasi-separated. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{X}$ is separated, then $M$ is separated. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Add more here, for example relative versions of the above. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      To prove this consider the following diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathcal{X} \ar[d]_ f \ar[r]_{\Delta _\mathcal {X}} &  \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \ar[d]^{f \times f} \\  M \ar[r]^{\Delta _ M} &  M \times M }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $f$ is a universal homeomorphism, we see that $f \times f$ is a universal homeomorphism. 
    <p>
      If $\mathcal{X}$ is separated, then $\Delta _\mathcal {X}$ is proper, hence $\Delta _\mathcal {X}$ is universally closed, hence $\Delta _ M$ is universally closed, hence $M$ is separated by Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/04Y0" data-tag="04Y0">67.40.9</a>. 
    <p>
      If $\mathcal{X}$ is quasi-separated, then $\Delta _\mathcal {X}$ is quasi-compact, hence $\Delta _ M$ is quasi-compact, hence $M$ is quasi-separated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>